(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-cp-exam-1-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What proportion of severe blindness from macular degeneration does the wet form account for?","choices":["About fifty percent","About twenty-five percent","About ten percent","About ninety percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the wet form progresses rapidly, because leaking new vessels cause rapid central vision loss, so it accounts for about ninety percent of severe blindness. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which statement describes the timing of acute inflammation?","choices":["Over minutes to hours only, beginning after a full 24 hours have elapsed since the injury","Over seconds, minutes, hours or days, beginning within seconds of tissue injury","Over days to months, beginning once macrophages arrive","Over weeks, beginning after antibody production starts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute inflammation begins immediately — within seconds — following injury of tissues. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What protects the outer third of the ear canal?","choices":["Ciliated epithelium sweeping debris outward","A keratin plug at the isthmus","Cerumen from ceruminous glands, and hair follicles","Mucus from goblet cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ceruminous glands produce protective cerumen, and the outer third of the canal also has protective hair follicles. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which cell types characterize chronic inflammation?","choices":["Basophils and mast cells only","Fibroblasts in the absence of leukocytes","Neutrophils and eosinophils predominantly, without mononuclear infiltrate","Macrophages, monocytes, lymphocytes and plasma cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic inflammation is characterized by macrophages, monocytes and mononuclear cells, with proliferation of blood vessels and connective tissue. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How do eccrine and apocrine sweat glands differ in secretion and function?","choices":["Eccrine secrete keratin and apocrine secrete melanin","Eccrine secrete water and electrolytes for evaporative cooling; apocrine, mainly axillary and anogenital, secrete protein and lipids as scent glands","Eccrine glands sit in the axilla and anogenital areas and secrete protein and fatty lipids; apocrine glands open onto the surface of the skin and secrete water and electrolytes for cooling the body","Both secrete sebum, differing only in the body regions they occupy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Eccrine glands open onto the skin surface and cool the body by evaporation; body odor comes from bacterial degradation of apocrine sweat, not from the sweat itself. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which virus is the typical cause of viral conjunctivitis?","choices":["Herpes simplex virus","Varicella zoster virus","Cytomegalovirus","Adenovirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — adenovirus is the viral cause of conjunctivitis, which can also be bacterial, allergic or toxic, and shows hyperemia without severe deep pain. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What defines telangiectasia at the tissue level?","choices":["Capillary rupture with blood deposited into the dermis","Dense inflammatory infiltration around dilated vessels","Proliferation of new vessels driven by release of vascular endothelial growth factor into the surrounding dermis over a prolonged period","Permanent dilatation and endothelial thinning of superficial dermal vessels, with NO inflammatory infiltrate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The dilated, thinned vessels are the capillaries, venules and arterioles of the superficial dermis, and the absence of inflammatory infiltrate is the discriminating feature. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"In the sequence of events of acute inflammation, which vascular event comes FIRST?","choices":["Increased vascular permeability","Hemoconcentration and stasis","Vasoconstriction","Vasodilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sequence opens with vasoconstriction, followed by vasodilation, increased vascular permeability, then hemoconcentration and stasis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What important caveat attaches to TNM (tumor, nodes, metastases) definitions?","choices":["They replace histological grading, which is no longer used","They are cancer-specific; for some cancers depth of invasion matters more than size","They are identical across every cancer, which is what makes the system universal","They apply only to epithelial malignancies and not to sarcomas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why a staging table applies to one named cancer rather than all. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which three interneuron types modulate the retinal signal before it reaches the ganglion cells?","choices":["Horizontal, amacrine and Purkinje cells","Rod, cone and ganglion cells","Bipolar, horizontal and amacrine cells","Bipolar, Müller and astrocyte cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — bipolar, horizontal and amacrine cells modulate the photoreceptor signal and route it to the ganglion cells, whose axons form the optic nerve. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which cancer patients may carry an inherited mutation of BRCA-1 or BRCA-2?","choices":["The majority of breast cancer patients","All patients with familial adenomatous polyposis","All patients with xeroderma pigmentosum","A minority of breast cancer patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a minority of breast cancer patients have an inherited mutation of these deoxyribonucleic acid repair genes. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes hypertrophy?","choices":["An increase in the number of cells; especially important in labile tissues such as epithelium and bone marrow","Replacement of one mature cell type by another; especially important at epithelial junctions","Autonomous proliferation of a clone of cells; especially important in glandular tissue","Enlargement of the individual cells; especially important in permanent tissues such as skeletal and cardiac muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it may also occur in other tissues as part of adaptive growth. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which description of a fissure is accurate?","choices":["A linear ulcer cracking the epidermis, from lost elasticity, severe dryness and mechanical tension","A superficial loss of the epidermis that spares the dermal-epidermal junction and everything beneath it, so no collagen is ever destroyed in the process of healing","A thickened plaque produced by chronic rubbing","A dilated superficial vessel without inflammatory infiltrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A fissure is a linear ulcer cracking the epidermis where lost elasticity, severe dryness and mechanical tension act on a thickened stratum corneum. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the classic symptom tetrad of Meniere disease?","choices":["Continuous vertigo lasting days, unilateral tinnitus, unilateral loss, and nystagmus","Vertigo lasting under a minute on head movement, with no hearing loss","High-frequency hearing loss with tinnitus and no vertigo","Episodic vertigo for hours, low-frequency tinnitus, low-tone loss, aural fullness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All four, and the LOW-frequency emphasis is what distinguishes the hearing loss from presbycusis or noise damage, which take the high frequencies first. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which pairing of endogenous and exogenous toxic agents in chronic inflammation is correct?","choices":["Endogenous — atherosclerosis; exogenous — particulates such as silica","Endogenous — bacterial infection; exogenous — viral infection","Endogenous — silica; exogenous — atherosclerosis","Endogenous — rheumatoid arthritis; exogenous — lupus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atherosclerosis is the endogenous example and particulates such as silica the exogenous one. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which description fits conductive hearing loss?","choices":["Defective sound transmission, in the external or middle ear","Central processing failure in the auditory cortex","Destruction of hair cells or auditory nerve fibers, in the inner ear","Failure of glutamate release at cranial nerve VIII"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The apparatus that carries sound inward has failed; the cochlea and nerve are intact. That anatomical split is what the tuning fork tests are detecting. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Recognition and attachment during phagocytosis are enhanced by the presence of what?","choices":["Serum factors known as opsonins","Histamine released from basophils","Fibrinogen deposited by the coagulation system","Bradykinin generated from plasma proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Opsonins such as immunoglobulin G and complement fragment C3b enhance recognition and attachment during phagocytosis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"From how many cells is the proliferating population of a neoplasm derived?","choices":["One cell, which underwent a genetic alteration","Many cells, each of which underwent an independent genetic alteration","Two cells, one parenchymal and one stromal","A variable number, depending on the tissue in which it arises"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the entire proliferating population derives from one cell that underwent a genetic alteration, so the neoplasm is clonal. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which receptors carry the sensations of touch and pressure?","choices":["Golgi tendon organs and muscle spindles","Langerhans cells and melanocytes","Meissner's corpuscles, Pacinian corpuscles and Merkel cells","Ruffini endings, Krause bulbs and free nerve endings only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Meissner's serve fine discriminative touch and low-frequency vibration, Pacinian respond to deep pressure and vibration, and Merkel cells serve light touch. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which mechanism underlies basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Uninhibited melanocyte growth at the dermal-epidermal junction","The Hedgehog pathway, after ultraviolet-induced mutation of basal keratinocytes","The tp53 tumor suppressor pathway, acting in the differentiated keratinocytes of the epidermis","Fibroblast growth factor receptor-3, in immature keratinocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ultraviolet-induced mutation of basal keratinocytes overactivates Hedgehog signaling and drives tumor growth in this slow-growing, rarely metastasizing cancer. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Why are nasal polyps classed as non-neoplastic?","choices":["No new tissue grows; the mucosa is edematous","They arise from cartilage rather than epithelium","They regress without treatment in every case","They are malignant but slow growing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nasal polyps are benign edematous masses of the mucous membranes of the sinus ostia or ethmoid air cells, not new neoplastic tissue. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why does psoriatic skin become dry?","choices":["The stratum corneum is lost entirely","Sebaceous glands overproduce sebum, which dries the surface","Epidermal cells fail to secrete lipids, producing xeroderma","Eccrine sweat glands are destroyed by inflammation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In psoriasis, epidermal cells do not secrete lipids, and that failure of secretion produces xeroderma, or dry skin. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which description fits the cones of the retina?","choices":["About 120 million, concentrated in the peripheral retina rather than the fovea","About 6 million, spread evenly across the whole retina","About 60 million, concentrated at the optic disc","About 6 million, concentrated in the fovea centralis within the macula"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — about 6 million cones, serving color vision and sharp acuity, are concentrated in the fovea centralis within the macula. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is characteristic of the exudate of acute inflammation?","choices":["The eosinophil","The lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The plasma cell"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute inflammation involves exudation of fluid, plasma, proteins and leukocytes, and the leukocyte of that exudate is the neutrophil. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes Helicobacter pylori?","choices":["A fungus that colonizes the gastric mucosa in immunosuppressed patients","A Gram-negative bacterium causing chronic gastritis","A Gram-positive bacterium that colonizes the stomach, causing acute ulceration and perforation","A virus that integrates its genome into gastric epithelial cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it colonizes the stomach and may lead to atrophic gastritis and intestinal metaplasia. It is the one bacterium among the microbial causes of cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which structures detect linear acceleration and gravitational position?","choices":["The organ of Corti","The three semicircular canals","The otolith organs","The cupula and ampulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They handle static and linear balance, while the semicircular canals handle rotation. The two systems together give the brain a complete picture of head position and movement. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why does cerumen impaction cause conductive rather than sensorineural loss?","choices":["It blocks sound delivery down the canal","It damages the outer hair cells","It stiffens the stapes footplate","It loads the tympanic membrane with fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cochlea is untouched, and the defect is purely one of delivery. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which property distinguishes the monocyte from the neutrophil in terms of sustained phagocytic capacity?","choices":["The monocyte has a multilobed nucleus permitting repeated division","The monocyte can replace its lysosomal contents","The monocyte does not require opsonization of its targets","The monocyte carries T cell receptors for direct antigen binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil cannot renew its lysosomes and dies after a few pathogens; the monocyte can replace its lysosomal contents and so keeps working. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A patient's inflamed tissue shows a leukocyte infiltrate dominated by cells with bi-lobed nuclei and pink-orange granules. Which process does this pattern most suggest?","choices":["A chronic autoimmune process","A humoral antibody response","An acute bacterial infection","An allergic or parasitic process"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bi-lobed nuclei with pink-orange granules identify eosinophils, which predominate in allergic reactions and parasitic infections. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of Meniere disease?","choices":["Defective endolymph resorption causes hydrops until micro-ruptures occur","Inflammatory swelling and vascular congestion of the labyrinth after a viral infection","Dislodged otoconia floating in the semicircular canals","Demyelination of the vestibular nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Endolymphatic hydrops is the lesion: fluid accumulates because it is not resorbed, the membranous labyrinth distends, and micro-ruptures follow. The episodic nature follows from that cycle. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why does a central chiasmal lesion spare nasal vision in both eyes?","choices":["The macula is spared by a dual blood supply","The lesion affects only one eye at a time","Only the crossing nasal fibers are cut, and they carry the temporal fields","The lesion damages the temporal retinal fibers instead, and those carry nasal vision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — only the nasal retinal fibers cross at the chiasm, and they carry the temporal fields; the temporal retinal fibers stay ipsilateral, so nasal vision survives. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Where does each step of vitamin D activation take place, beginning in the skin?","choices":["Cholecalciferol is produced in the liver, then hydroxylated in the skin and finally in the kidneys","Cholecalciferol is produced in the skin and hydroxylated entirely within the kidneys in a single step","Cholecalciferol forms in the skin under ultraviolet B light, then is hydroxylated in the liver and then the kidneys to calcitriol","Calcitriol is produced directly in the skin under ultraviolet B light and requires no further modification in either the liver or the kidneys at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The skin only starts the process; two further hydroxylation steps in separate organs are needed before the hormone is active. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which layer forms the semi-impermeable barrier of the skin?","choices":["The stratum basale, a single layer of rapidly dividing columnar keratinocytes at the base","The stratum granulosum, 3 to 5 layers of diamond-shaped cells","The papillary dermis, a thin superficial connective tissue layer","The stratum corneum, 20 to 30 layers of keratin and dead keratinocytes cemented together"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The stratum corneum, the outermost layer, packs 20 to 30 layers of keratin and dead keratinocytes into a semi-impermeable barrier. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which description of petechiae is accurate?","choices":["Minor bleeding into the dermis, such as from capillary rupture, 1 to 2 millimeters or less","Permanent dilatation of the superficial dermal vessels, measuring 1 to 2 millimeters across the surface of the skin","Increased melanin production in the basal layer, up to 5 millimeters","Mast cell histamine release with plasma leakage, up to 5 millimeters"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Petechiae are non-blanchable pinpoint deposits of blood in the dermis, 1 to 2 millimeters or less, from minor bleeding such as capillary rupture, infection, thrombocytopenia or vasculitis. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which statement about neutrophils is correct?","choices":["They present antigen to T cells after phagocytosis","They are the predominant inflammatory cell in allergic reactions and in parasitic infection","They respond first to microbial infection, and their mass death forms pus","They mature in the thymus and carry T cell receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are first responders to microbial infection, and their activity and death in large numbers forms pus. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"In which cavity does seeding most commonly occur?","choices":["The subarachnoid space","The peritoneal cavity","The pericardial cavity","The pleural cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — seeding most commonly occurs in the peritoneal cavity; the pericardial and pleural cavities and the subarachnoid space can also be seeded. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is a metastasis?","choices":["Local invasion of a cancer into the tissue immediately surrounding it","A benign nodule that develops in the tissue draining a cancer","A portion of a cancer that has migrated from the primary site to other sites","A second, unrelated primary cancer arising in another organ"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the primary remains where it began; the metastasis is what has left. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of the most common, senile form of cataract?","choices":["Progressive insoluble aggregation and deposition of lens crystallin proteins","Osmotic swelling of the lens driven by sorbitol accumulating inside it","Rupture of the lens capsule after blunt trauma","Chronic ultraviolet-driven oxidative damage alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — senile cataract, the most common form, results from progressive insoluble aggregation and deposition of lens crystallin proteins, opacifying the lens. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why does asymmetrical vestibular input produce a false sense of motion?","choices":["The brain reads the mismatch as motion","Cerebral perfusion falls on one side","The otoliths dissolve","The cochlea sends conflicting auditory signals"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Balance is a comparison, so unequal input is indistinguishable from turning. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which description fits a chalazion?","choices":["An acute, tender, focal abscess of a gland sitting at the eyelid margin","A cholesterol-filled plaque along the nasal eyelid","Redundant lid skin with prolapse of orbital fat","A non-tender, sterile lipogranuloma of a blocked meibomian gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sterile and granulomatous is what separates it from an infection. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does prolonged antibiotic use predispose to FUNGAL otitis externa?","choices":["It thins the canal skin directly","It removes competing bacteria","It raises the canal pH to favor fungi","It damages the tympanic membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prolonged antibiotic use suppresses the bacteria, and Aspergillus niger or Candida albicans then take over as fungal otitis externa. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which description matches the optics of myopia?","choices":["The globe is normal but the lens has lost elasticity","The axial globe is too long, so the focal point falls in front of the retina","The axial globe is too short, so the focal point falls behind the retina","The globe is normal but the cornea is irregularly curved"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an elongated globe overshoots, focusing short of the retina. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What are the four grades of neoplastic differentiation, from most to least resemblance?","choices":["Anaplasia, poorly differentiated, moderately differentiated, well differentiated","In situ, microinvasive, invasive, metastatic","Stage I, stage II, stage III, stage IV","Well differentiated, moderately differentiated, poorly differentiated, anaplasia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — differentiation runs from close resemblance (well) through intermediate and poor resemblance to anaplasia, the lack of differentiation. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does it mean, structurally, for lymph nodes to be MATTED?","choices":["They move together as one mass","They are tender and mobile","They are smaller than normal","They fluctuate in size with infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Matted nodes have lost their individual capsules and move together as one mass, and persistent rubbery or matted nodes in older adults require assessment to rule out lymphoma. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Beyond phagocytosis, what is the monocyte's role in the immune response?","choices":["Secreting histamine to open the capillary bed","Producing the fibrous network that traps exudate","Presenting pathogen pieces to T cells so it can be recognized again and killed","Restoring the immune system to its normal function once the infection has resolved"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After phagocytosis, monocytes present pieces of pathogens to T cells so that the pathogens may be recognized again and killed. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the bone remodeling sequence in otosclerosis?","choices":["Demineralization of the ossicles from chronic inflammation","Fibrous scarring of the round window membrane","Osteoblastic overgrowth of dense cortical bone without resorption","Osteoclastic resorption, then spongy osteoid replacement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Abnormal osteoclastic bone resorption is followed by hypervascular spongy osteoid replacement around the otic capsule and stapes footplate, which then fixes the footplate. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How is the avascular cornea oxygenated?","choices":["By the choroidal circulation behind it","By direct contact with air and tears","By diffusion from the limbal blood vessels alone","By the aqueous humor circulating through the anterior chamber"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the cornea is entirely avascular, so it is oxygenated through direct contact with air and tears rather than by any blood supply. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is metaplasia?","choices":["Change of one cell type to another under chronic irritation or injury","Disordered growth of epithelial cells with variation in size and shape and loss of architectural orientation","Enlargement of an organ because its individual cells enlarge under increased demand","Autonomous growth of an abnormal mass of tissue derived from a single genetically altered cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it involves a change in differentiation. Bladder transitional epithelium becoming squamous is the standard example. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which description of lichenification is accurate?","choices":["Separation of the epidermis from the dermis with accumulation of fluid between the two layers, forming a large blister that lifts the surface away","Slowing of keratinocyte division with elastin degradation","Long-term scratching or rubbing in an itch-scratch cycle, causing hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis in thick plaques without scaling","Loss of melanocytes across a circumscribed area"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The itch-scratch cycle drives hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis into firm, thick plaques without scaling, as in lichen simplex chronicus. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What do initiators do in chemical carcinogenesis?","choices":["They cause permanent damage to deoxyribonucleic acid","They cause sustained or enhanced proliferation of already-damaged cells","They repair damage already caused by another carcinogen","They trigger apoptosis in cells with damaged genomes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — permanence is what distinguishes initiation from promotion. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Besides cervical cancer, which malignancies is high-risk human papillomavirus associated with?","choices":["Retinoblastoma and osteosarcoma","Anal, vulvar, vaginal, penile and oropharyngeal cancer","Hepatocellular carcinoma and gastric adenocarcinoma","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and certain B cell lymphomas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — high-risk human papillomavirus is associated with all five in addition to cervical cancer, the oropharyngeal one being a squamous cell carcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is iris bombé?","choices":["Forward displacement of the iris against the cornea","Backward displacement of the iris against the lens","Atrophy of the iris stroma after an acute attack","Adhesion of the iris to the corneal endothelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — in angle closure, pupil dilation pushes the iris forward against the cornea, and this iris bombé blocks aqueous outflow totally so pressure spikes. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which description of Beau's lines is accurate?","choices":["Impaired keratin synthesis, associated with deficiency anemia","A halt of keratin production causing transverse grooves, with illness, stress, nail injury or malnourishment","Overgrowth of connective tissue within the nail bed, associated with chronic liver disease and with diabetes mellitus in particular","Increased capillary density, associated with lung disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beau's lines are horizontal or transverse grooves caused by a halt of keratin production, associated with illness, stress, nail injury and malnourishment. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"On the dysplasia spectrum, what separates severe dysplasia from invasive cancer?","choices":["The presence of mitoses, which appear only once invasion has occurred","Involvement of the regional lymph nodes","The basement membrane","The degree of nuclear enlargement, which is greater in invasive cancer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — carcinoma in situ has not invaded through the basement membrane, while invasive cancer has broken through it. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which change in the vitreous underlies rhegmatogenous retinal detachment?","choices":["It solidifies, pressing the retina more firmly against the pigment epithelium","It becomes vascularized, bleeding into the subretinal space","It thickens and opacifies, blocking light before it reaches the retina","It liquefies, so fluid tracks through a break into the subretinal space"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — once liquefied, vitreous fluid can pass through a full-thickness retinal tear into the subretinal space and peel the neurosensory retina off the pigment epithelium. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A lesion of the optic tract produces which defect?","choices":["Bitemporal hemianopsia","Monocular blindness","Central scotoma with preserved peripheral vision","Contralateral homonymous hemianopsia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an optic tract lesion, from stroke, tumor or demyelination, causes contralateral homonymous hemianopsia, the loss of the opposite visual field. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How are dermatofibroma and lipoma characterized in the tissue?","choices":["Both are malignant proliferations of keratinocytes","Dermatofibroma is keratin-filled and lipoma is fluid-filled","Dermatofibroma is a benign overgrowth of fibrous tissue in the dermis; lipoma is a benign enclosed capsule of adipocytes","Dermatofibroma is a benign enclosed capsule of adipocytes; lipoma is a fibrous tissue overgrowth within the dermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dermatofibroma is a benign overgrowth of fibrous tissue in the dermis, and lipoma is a benign enclosed capsule of adipocytes. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which statement about histamine in the inflammatory response is correct?","choices":["The final mediator released during the whole response, causing fibrosis and the formation of dense scar tissue at the site","The first mediator of the response, dilating arterioles and increasing capillary and venule permeability","A chemotactic factor drawing neutrophils to the site","An opsonin that coats foreign material for recognition"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Histamine is the first mediator of the initial response, dilating arterioles and increasing capillary and venule permeability. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Histamine and serotonin share which pair of effects?","choices":["Chemotaxis and opsonization","Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability","Pain and fibrin deposition","Antibody production and antigen presentation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Histamine dilates arterioles and increases capillary and venule permeability, and serotonin likewise causes vasodilation and increased vascular permeability. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which physical law explains why a small septal deviation causes disproportionate obstruction?","choices":["Boyle's law","Poiseuille's law","Fick's law of diffusion","Laplace's law"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. By Poiseuille's law even small airway narrowing dramatically increases resistance, so a modest deviation can produce unilateral resistance and chronic nasal obstruction. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
